From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 11:50:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] lockscreen: present frames from an out-of-process renderer

While a session is locked, ext-session-lock-v1 presents exactly one client's
surface and Wayland subsurfaces must come from the same wl_client, so a
screensaver in another process cannot draw underneath the lock. Add an optional,
build-flagged host that lets a separate process render off-screen and hand the
lock client a buffer fd over an AF_UNIX SOCK_SEQPACKET socket; the lock imports
it with wl_shm and presents it on its lock surface.

The renderer is never code in the lock process, so failure containment is
structural: a crash, hang, GPU error, bad descriptor or malformed message can
only make the lock drop back to its own Cairo prompt. The lock client is never
signalled by this code; only the frame-source child is reaped, with backoff.
Input hides the frames and returns the prompt (the source is told to stop); the
frames return after the idle delay.

Off by default: without -Dlock_framesrc=enabled the locker is unchanged, and
even when compiled in it is inert unless SINGULARITY_LOCK_RENDERER names a frame
source. Includes a deterministic frame source (memfd, no GPU) and a headless
labwc test that covers frame presentation, input dismissal, kill -9, a hung
source, a short buffer, and a source that dies on a GPU error.

Assisted-by: opencode:deepseek-flash
AI-Scope: implemented the frame-source host, wire protocol, lock integration, meson flag, test frame source and headless labwc test from the operator's P1 design brief
